   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property tax law, in relation to authorizing\nmunicipalities to restrict the definitions of \"commercial purpose or use\" and\n\"mixed-use property\"",
    "basePrintNo": "S5254",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5254-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 53,
       "fullName": "Rachel May",
       "imgName": "1057_Rachel_May.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1237,
       "sessionMemberId": 1235,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "MAY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §485-a, RPT L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Tax Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5254\n\nSPONSOR: BRESLIN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the real property tax law, in relation\nto authorizing municipalities to restrict the definitions of \"commercial\npurpose or use\" and \"mixed-use property\"\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nTo provide municipalities utilizing the § 485-a property tax exemption\nfor mixed-use development authority to better define what an acceptable\nmixed-use or commercial purpose is for a project to be eligible.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends subdivision 2 of § 485-a of the Real Property Tax Law\nto give municipalities further authority to define what is a \"commercial\npurpose or use\" and \"mixed-use\" property to determine which properties\nor projects are eligible to receive a § 485-a property tax abatement.\n\nSection 2: Establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\n§ 485-a of the Real Property Tax Law was enacted in 2002 to encourage\nredevelopment of non-residential properties in New York State. It allows\nmunicipalities to offer a 12 year partial exemption of property and\nspecial ad valorem taxes to non-residential properties converted to a\nmix of residential and commercial uses.\n\nUnfortunately, the current statute does not provide a clear definition\nof \"commercial purpose or use\" and \"mixed-use\". This has allowed some\nprojects to receive a § 485-a exemption without truly including a\ncommercial or mixed-use element in the property. An example of this is a\nproperty attempting to apply for the exemption with the only commercial\nuse being vending machines in the lobby. While local assessors do not\nwish to provide exemptions to these types of projects, State law is\nvague on what qualifies as an acceptable use.\n\nThis legislation would give localities utilizing § 485-a additional\nauthority to more strictly define what qualifies as an acceptable\ncommercial or mixed-use. Allowing localities this authority will help\nreign in abuses under § 485-a, while giving them the flexibility to\ndetermine what is best for their particular program. The goal is to\nensure that § 485-a exemptions are going to projects that are truly\nmixed-use and beneficial to their communities.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE:\n\nImmediately\n\n",
